Advanced Frontend Technology Requirements

Technology Selection

Do not blindly upgrade the application’s existing frontend framework or core dependencies.

First inspect the existing frontend stack and preserve compatibility with it.

Use the most modern, production-ready technologies and libraries that are compatible with the current application.

Do not introduce dependencies simply because they are new. Every additional dependency must have a clear purpose and should materially improve the required UX.

If the application is React-based, strongly consider the following technologies where appropriate:

* Three.js
* React Three Fiber
* Drei
* Motion / Framer Motion
* a lightweight WebGL-compatible particle solution
* post-processing/bloom effects where useful

Use equivalent modern technologies if the existing framework is not React.

3D Rendering

The Deployment Topology must be a genuine interactive 3D/WebGL visualization, not a collection of CSS shapes pretending to be 3D.

Use hardware-accelerated rendering where practical.

The topology should support:

* smooth camera movement
* zoom
* pan/orbit where appropriate
* depth
* perspective
* dynamic node positioning
* animated connections
* particle/energy flow
* subtle lighting
* glass/crystal materials
* hover interaction
* selection
* animated transitions
* responsive resizing

The visual result should remain elegant and readable rather than becoming a technical 3D demo.

Crystal / Glass Rendering

Use physically-inspired or visually convincing translucent materials where practical.

The central Kubernetes object and Deployment nodes should have:

* transparent/translucent surfaces
* subtle refraction-style appearance
* luminous edges
* internal gradients
* controlled bloom
* subtle lighting
* depth
* slight parallax

Avoid excessive transparency that makes text difficult to read.

Do not use heavy post-processing effects that significantly reduce performance.

Energy Flow

The connection between the central Kubernetes object and Deployment nodes should use animated particles or another GPU-efficient technique.

Particles should:

* originate from the central Kubernetes node
* travel toward the Deployment
* fade smoothly
* use staggered timing
* support multiple simultaneous connections
* change semantic color based on Deployment state
* avoid excessive particle counts

Healthy:
cyan/green.

Updating:
blue.

Warning:
amber.

Failed:
red/pink.

Stopped:
muted/no active particle flow.

Prefer GPU-friendly rendering for this effect rather than creating hundreds of DOM elements.

UI Motion

Use a dedicated motion system rather than individually inventing animation timings throughout the application.

Define reusable motion tokens for:

* hover
* selection
* panel transitions
* layout changes
* topology transitions
* camera movement
* filtering
* search
* operation progress
* success
* failure

Motion should be smooth, deliberate and meaningful.

Do not animate every element continuously.

Real-Time Updates

The visual state must be driven by real Kubernetes state.

Where the existing application already provides WebSocket infrastructure, reuse it.

When Kubernetes state changes:

2/2 Ready
    ↓
1/2 Ready
    ↓
2/2 Ready

the visualization should animate the actual state transition.

Do not fake operational progress based on arbitrary timers.

Topology Layout

The topology must dynamically calculate node positions.

Do not hardcode positions for specific Deployments.

Support at least:

* 1–5 Deployments
* 6–10 Deployments
* 11–20 Deployments
* 20+ Deployments

Use adaptive radial/orbital layouts or an equivalent algorithm.

Avoid node collisions and overlapping labels.

The layout should remain stable when possible so that small state changes do not cause the entire topology to jump.

When filtering/searching, animate nodes into their new positions rather than abruptly recreating the scene.

Performance

Treat performance as a first-class requirement.

Avoid:

* unnecessary React re-renders
* excessive DOM animation
* excessive WebGL objects
* unnecessary post-processing
* recreating materials/geometries every render
* uncontrolled particle counts

Reuse geometries and materials where possible.

Use memoization and appropriate state isolation.

If necessary, reduce visual effects dynamically as object count increases.

The UI must remain responsive with approximately 20+ Deployments and many Pods.

WebGL Fallback

Provide a graceful fallback if WebGL is unavailable or disabled.

The application must remain fully usable.

Fallback options may include:

* a high-quality 2D topology representation
* reduced visual mode
* the Hex Tile View

Do not show a broken canvas or blank screen.

Accessibility

Respect:

prefers-reduced-motion

When reduced motion is enabled:

* disable/reduce particle movement
* reduce camera animation
* remove excessive pulsing
* reduce transition duration
* preserve all information and functionality

Do not rely solely on animation or color to communicate state.

Visual QA

After implementation, perform a dedicated visual QA pass.

Check the UI at:

* normal desktop resolution
* large monitor
* laptop resolution
* smaller browser window
* 5 Deployments
* 10 Deployments
* 15 Deployments
* 20+ Deployments
* mixed healthy/updating/failed/stopped states

Specifically verify:

* topology readability
* node spacing
* particle performance
* glow intensity
* text contrast
* animation smoothness
* camera behavior
* hover interaction
* filtering
* search
* responsive behavior
* WebGL fallback
* reduced-motion behavior

Do not consider the implementation complete merely because the functional tests pass. Perform a visual refinement pass and remove any interaction, spacing, animation, or rendering issues that make the screen feel like a prototype.
